(viii) Distributions are paid only after all legal and contractual obligations have been met and payments on more senior capital instruments have been made. This means that there are no preferential distributions, including in respect of other elements classified as the highest quality issued capital;
(ix) It is the paid-up capital that takes the first and proportionately greatest share of any losses as they occur. Within the highest quality capital, each instrument absorbs losses on a going concern basis proportionately and pari passu with all the others. In cases where capital instruments have a permanent write-down feature, this criterion is still deemed to be met by common shares; (x) The paid-up amount is classified as equity capital (i.e., not recognised as a liability) for determining balance sheet insolvency;
